// os/vscode/keybindings.windows.jsonc — the Windows-only additions.
// Merge these into `%APPDATA%\Code\User\keybindings.json` **in addition to**
// everything in `keybindings.jsonc`. Nothing here belongs on the Mac.
//
// Why anything is Windows-only at all: the Claude Code extension's own
// shortcuts are the two chords Windows itself refuses to hand over.
//
//   | extension default | on macOS | on Windows                          |
//   | ----------------- | -------- | ----------------------------------- |
//   | focus / blur      | Cmd+Esc  | Ctrl+Esc = **opens the Start menu**  |
//   | open in new tab   | ⌘⇧Esc    | Ctrl+Shift+Esc = **Task Manager**    |
//
// Both Windows chords are claimed by the shell before any application sees
// them, so the VSCode_Win layer emits `Shift+F17` / `Shift+F18` instead
// (positions 40 and 39), and these entries point those at the same
// commands. The macOS layer emits the extension's real defaults and needs
// no entries — which is exactly why this is a separate file.
//
// Command IDs and `when` clauses below were read straight out of the
// installed extension's `package.json` (`contributes.keybindings`,
// anthropic.claude-code 2.1.250) on 2026-08-28 — they are no longer the
// unverified guesses this file carried before. Re-check after a major
// extension update: Ctrl+Shift+P -> "Preferences: Open Default Keyboard
// Shortcuts (JSON)" and search "claude".
[
  // ---- Shift+F18 (position 39) — open Claude in a new tab / new session.
  { "key": "shift+f18", "command": "claude-vscode.editor.open",
    "when": "!config.claudeCode.useTerminal" },

  // ---- Shift+F17 (position 40) — focus <-> blur the Claude input. Was
  // also the RAlt *tap* on the Windows base layer until 2026-08-31, when
  // that thumb went back to being a plain Alt key. This is one chord and THREE
  // commands: the extension discriminates by `when`, so all three have to
  // be bound or the toggle only works in one direction. `focus` and `blur`
  // cover the normal (panel/sidebar) mode; `terminal.open.keyboard` is the
  // variant used when `claudeCode.useTerminal` is on. Binding all three
  // makes the key correct under either setting.
  { "key": "shift+f17", "command": "claude-vscode.focus",
    "when": "!config.claudeCode.useTerminal && editorTextFocus" },
  { "key": "shift+f17", "command": "claude-vscode.blur",
    "when": "!config.claudeCode.useTerminal && !editorTextFocus" },
  { "key": "shift+f17", "command": "claude-vscode.terminal.open.keyboard",
    "when": "config.claudeCode.useTerminal" }
]
